proof=load('PANEL_PROJECTION_PROOF');p=proof[0];new=f'''## Another substantial candidate: panel moments

The current point adapter stores three complex phase/dipole tables at eight Gauss nodes per panel. At 4k/k=40 these tables occupy {p['point_phase_bytes']/2**20:.2f} MiB. Preprojecting source and target plane waves onto the two P1 endpoint functions would store {p['proposed_source_target_projection_bytes']/2**20:.2f} MiB for those moments: **six times less for this payload**. Normals are constant on a linear panel, and derivative moments follow from the sum of endpoint moments divided by panel length.

`panel_projection_proof.py` checked the source charge/dipole/derivative and target value/normal-gradient/derivative identities for real k, lossy k and an all-near case; the largest identity difference was {max(max(v['relative_max_identity_errors'].values()) for v in proof):.3g}. This is an algebraic/storage feasibility check, **not a new full operator or a measured solver RAM/time saving**. A complete adapter must preserve the translations, signs, panel assignment and all four Galerkin operators. It must construct the moments in bounded tiles rather than allocating the old tables first. The all-near fallback has zero far translations and can skip unused far-phase storage altogether.

This is a concrete independent implementation task for Claude, alongside the preconditioner investigation. It can be combined with local hierarchical compression.

'''
s=s.replace('## What should happen next',new+'## What should happen next');assert '<!--' not in s;REPORT.write_text(s);print('Rendered report tables')
